this morning on the dish from small town  girl to big city chef and back again  that describes kelsey barnard clark and  her journey from alabama to the culinary  institute of america to some of new  york's top restaurants our very own  jamie wax paid her a visit good morning  jamie good morning my friend that's  right clark is now back home blending  all that experience with a healthy dose  of southern hospitality at her  restaurant and in her new cookbook i  talked with her about that and the  cooking show win that put her on the  culinary map  fried chicken and champagne is  definitely like the last meal i would  eat before i die probably maybe throw  some caviar on there just in case why  not of all the many places chef kelsey  barnard clark has cooked the very finest  meal you might get is in her favorite  spot her home  but what a beautiful southern home thank  you it's where we sat down to an opulent  spread of southern cuisine  sweet corn with paprika cheese and  citrus southern biscuits shrimp with  succotash  deviled eggs playfully called wicked  chickens  one time someone made a joke to me they  said do you know why we call them wicked  chickens i was like no  and they said because wicked chickens  make the best deviled eggs i was like oh  okay  i am a huge deviled egg fam this is by  far one of the very most delicious i've  ever had the smokiness of the brisket  and then the brininess of the okra and  then you've got a hit of pimento cheese  which is just perfect it creates this  profile that is a deviled egg but it's  it's so much more you can't get more  classic southern than okra and pimento  cheese the other thing i notice about  your cooking  is it's not just the profile of these  foods that are beautiful but it's so  much about texture a lot of southern  recipes lack herbs  they like acid and they lack texture a  lot of times you'll see me adding  crunchy things and there's always herbs  on everything i do i'm a big big fan of  herbs this is one of my favorite meals  like to eat personally i ate it for  lunch yesterday i was making it actually  it's succotash and then i love shrimp so  much so you top it with shrimp if there  was a dish that kind of described me it  would probably be this  and a hallmark of southern cuisine  perfectly fried chicken this was taught  to me all right i can't wait because  then you hear that that's what you want  to hear  yeah oh i mean so juicy  and it's it's so beautifully crispy and  fried it's that marriage where you can't  tell  where the skin ends and the crust begins  clark grew up in alabama showing an  interest in food at a very early age  after graduating from the culinary  institute of america she began cooking  in some of the most respected kitchens  in new york  when i was working at cafe boulud i was  responsible for family meal which in our  industry is cooking for each other right  i was working under gavin cason and he  was executive chef and he was like i  want you to cook your food quit cooking  everybody else says cook your food and i  was like  oh okay i gotta cook gumbo they call my  mom's like what's the gummy recipe i  don't remember so she had to type it out  and i coat gumbo and then it just was  one of those things i would just  remember making the roux in that kitchen  being  kind of going this is crazy and why  didn't i ever write this down this is a  moment every single  southern  home chef will  relate to in this story right now  after her time in new york with  everywhere to choose from  she chose to continue her vision back  home in dothan alabama my plan was to go  to new orleans and so i was coming home  for just a breather because i was burned  out truly i said you know what i'm going  to cater for a little bit because the  caterer that i'd worked for he was my  mentor and he had passed away and there  was this huge void for fine dining  catering here and you know rest is  history didn't leave i ended up getting  here i was fully booked for a year in  about a month and there was just this  wow oh yeah and i had this moment of  like why am i so acting like i'm so  against my town when they are so  unbelievably supportive of me  and i think that that's just where this  all started order up hayden  from there she opened her restaurant kbc  you know when i was in high school we  didn't there was nothing not a single  business here attracting other  businesses to a now thriving downtown i  mean this downtown area and this main  street survived the pandemic yes which  is  i mean what a blessing to even be able  to say that my restaurant survived it  which most of my friends didn't  when the opportunity came to compete on  top chef it meant she had to leave  behind her husband and then 10 month old  son for the duration  the sacrifice paid off and clark took  home the big prize  kelsey  you are top chef  i think for me it was it was a life  lesson more than it was about the food  and and it was just a wonderful  experience because when in your life do  you get to go somewhere  and anyone you've ever respected admired  wanted to learn from is  actually doing it with you for 10 weeks  all right  this is your last two and she's used the  exposure she got from her wind to spread  the message that southern cooking and  hospitality should be inclusive of  everyone it is incredibly important for  me  at kbc it is always a perfect picture of  what culture should look like and i  think that that's the really cool thing  about this new era we're in  is southern hospitality reaching farther  than we thought it would reach is it  important to you that you're a major  voice in that oh it is probably the most  important thing to me the lessons and  recipes she's drawn from southern  culture are now the basis for her new  cookbook southern grit and i've been a  collector of cookbooks since i was 12  years old so that's really what i always  think back on when i see this book now  on my shelf with all the other ones i'm  like whoa i wrote a book read a cookbook  someone's reading this you know you  don't want a ton of breading but it's  breaded enough when it's no longer wet  the most fun thing about cooking is  teaching people and hopefully inspiring  someone to love to cook one day  and for clark staying inspired herself  is everything i haven't even gotten  started yet that's how i really feel to  me  there's never like a stopping point you  got to keep getting better and pushing  yourself and learning more and got to  keep going finding a new thing and then  getting really good at it and that's  sort of  always my goal being in the moment being  in the moment and  never settling for  just now  uh we have a beautiful spread from her  literally we've got this pimento cheese  cheese straws which are such a great  southern appetizer and finger food and  then pepper jelly and it's all meant to  go together the cheese fries in that's  it then you got the sparkling sangria  she's just she is a wonderful advocate  for diversity in southern cooking for  the complicated history of southern  cooking but she's just a great chef  awesome
 

